+# Privacy by Design — The Daily Grind website
+
+This document records how the site handles visitor data and what to check before changing it.
+
+## Data inventory
+
+| Data | Collected? | Notes |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Analytics / behavioural tracking | No | No analytics, pixels, or session replay of any kind. |
+| Request logs (IP, user agent, URL) | No | `server.mjs` writes nothing per request. |
+| Query strings | Ignored | The server routes on path only. `privacy.js` removes campaign/click IDs (`utm_*`, `gclid`, `fbclid`, …) from the address bar. |
+| Cookies | None set by this page | Any session cookie for sign-in is set by the account backend; logout clears it via `Clear-Site-Data`. |
+| `localStorage` / `sessionStorage` | Not used | Cleared on logout as a safeguard. |
+| Image metadata | Stripped | The hero images contain no EXIF/XMP/GPS segments (only an ICC colour profile). |
+
+## Third-party audit
+
+Audited 2026-10-30.
+
+| Dependency | Before | Now | Reason |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Unsplash image CDN (`images.unsplash.com`) | Hero image loaded on every page view, exposing visitor IP, user agent, and Referer to the CDN | Self-hosted in `assets/img/` (Unsplash License permits this) | Page views no longer go to a third party |
+| `statikk` via `pnpm dlx` | Unpinned package downloaded and executed at every start; could only set CSP | Replaced by the zero-dependency `server.mjs` | Removes a supply-chain risk and allows the full header set |
+
+The page now makes **no third-party requests**. The CSP (`default-src 'none'` with `'self'` only) blocks any new ones until it is deliberately changed.
+
+### Before adding a third party
+
+1. Write down what personal data it receives (IP and Referer count) and why it's needed.
+2. Prefer self-hosting (fonts, images, scripts) over loading from a CDN.
+3. If it must be third-party, load it only after explicit opt-in consent and never when `navigator.globalPrivacyControl` is `true`.
+4. Add the narrowest possible origin to the CSP in **both** `server.mjs` and the `<meta>` fallback in `index.html`, and update this file.
+
+## Security and privacy headers (`server.mjs`)
+
+- **Content-Security-Policy**: `default-src 'none'`, same-origin scripts, styles, images, and form actions only. No inline code, `frame-ancestors 'none'`, and Trusted Types enforced.
+- **Referrer-Policy: same-origin**: no Referer is sent off-site.
+- **Permissions-Policy**: camera, microphone, geolocation, payment, sensors, and other powerful features are all denied, plus the `browsing-topics` and `interest-cohort` ad APIs.
+- **Cross-Origin-Opener/Embedder/Resource-Policy, Origin-Agent-Cluster**: isolate the page from other origins.
+- **X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options, X-DNS-Prefetch-Control: off, X-Permitted-Cross-Domain-Policies: none**.
+- **Cache-Control: no-store** on HTML, because pages show signed-in navigation.
+- **Strict-Transport-Security** and `upgrade-insecure-requests`: sent only when `NODE_ENV=production`, which should mean serving over TLS.
+
+## Secure data handling
+
+- Logout is a same-origin `POST`, not a link. This means it can't be fired by prefetching or by a cross-site `<img>`. The server rejects cross-site posts and replies with `Clear-Site-Data: "cache", "cookies", "storage"`.
+- The server serves an exact allowlist built at startup (`/`, `/index.html`, `/.well-known/gpc.json`, `/assets/**`), so source files, dotfiles, and path traversal are never reachable.
+- `/.well-known/gpc.json` declares that the site honors [Global Privacy Control](https://globalprivacycontrol.org/). The footer confirms this to visitors who send the signal.
+- Off-site links automatically get `rel="noopener noreferrer"`.
